Z||000014||RiceBurner515||12-04-2000||10:43 PM||||have some of your friends help and have them push it as fast as they can and try pop-starting it in 2nd gear, that is what i did when my 2.5 wouldn't start, but it was due to a loose alt. belt. lol||||reg||1
Z||000015||2M4 Dale||12-05-2000||04:00 AM||||I freed up a 41 Plymouth once that had been sitting for about 30 years with Auto Tranny Fluid.  I just poured it into the sparkplug holes and let it soak for about three days. Man Oh Man did it smoke like h$ll when I got it started...lol.||||reg||6
Z||000016||chrissherman||12-06-2000||12:54 AM||||Darn,the moter has not poped lose yet,by rocking it in 4th gear.Today I made a braker bar out of an old box end and a pipe.eny more ideas.I want to get the car driving before the snow hits the fan in juneau||||reg||8
Z||000017||Indiana_resto_guy||12-06-2000||08:56 AM||||Don't force it too much, don't want to break it!<BR>Rocking in 1st or 2nd will give better results as opposed to 4th.||||reg||1
Z||000018||StuGood||12-06-2000||09:13 AM||||Did you loosen or remove the accessory drive belts (alternator belt, etc.) yet?  I suppose a seized alternator or water pump could be part of the trouble.  Notice I always go for the cheap, simple stuff first?  Simple minds.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>After that, if you can't make it turn over with a wrench, I suppose I'd try pulling the starter out; then pull the flywheel cover off and inspect, then pull the distributor to make myself feel better...  but if the oil pump were seized - well, what Ogre said above.<P>After trying all that, if it won't go, I suppose it could be something going on in the clutch/trans., but it would mean the cradle is coming out in any case.  JMHO.       ||||reg||5
